{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790266127,"nonce":"aBGiXTdiFCuQGPtd1B1Zrph0BBE=","title":"zylua mandelbrot.lua kernel slower once the warm-up no longer prebuilds release facts","message":"With 94bf7088 (release facts shipped in the library snapshot), crates/zyntax_lua/benchmarks/mandelbrot.lua is consistently slower than main dea6d08b on the NUC (x86_64, LLVM on): kernel elapsed about 0.17 to 0.21 s on main against 0.24 to 0.30 s on the candidate over 30+ interleaved runs, wall 322 to 336 ms against 402 to 438 ms (medians), user CPU 0.46 to 0.51 s against 0.64 to 0.67 s. Seen at load averages from 2.9 to 10.\n\nThe gap is gone with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 (both about 0.19 s) and with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_LAZY=1 (both about 0.10 s), and stays with ZYLUA_LLVM=0 (0.17 against 0.25 s). Output is identical. GC activity (ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC), the release facts (ZYNTAX_TRACE_DROP owned-storage list), the number of lazy compiles (216 each) and the OSR event sequence (outlined resume points at the same sites, resume2 and resume3 promoted to LLVM) match between the two. The one scheduling difference is that the warm-up thread no longer spends its first 10 to 20 ms building facts while main waits, so the interpreter reaches the hot loop earlier and the warm-up compiles concurrently with it. No other Lua or Python micro kernel moved outside its spread.\n\nReproduce: for k in 1 2 3 4; do zylua mandelbrot.lua | grep elapsed; ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 zylua mandelbrot.lua | grep elapsed; done, on both binaries.","files":null}]}